  "inputs": "Every model is a normalized probability distribution BEFORE detection. Count propagates classical path multiplicities. Alternate and ordinary are the two fixed-label signed eighth-root evaluations in check_optical_walk_records.phase_sweep. The 22 listed site/polarization pairs include two unreachable outer-edge modes. Pure count has return 63/256 under equal efficiency and a setting-independent detected return under any common fixed efficiencies.",
  "proof": "Write h_j=1 at the centre, zero elsewhere. For fixed efficiencies e_j>=0, positive total collected mass at both settings, and component probabilities P_sj, the upper bound at 0 deg gives sum e_j(h_j-U)P_0j<=0 and the lower bound at 135 deg gives sum e_j(L-h_j)P_135j<=0. Their positive combination defines D_j=lambda(h_j-U)P_0j +(L-h_j)P_135j with lambda=L/(4U). Exact signs below show that at w=0 all coefficients are nonnegative with both count and alternate strictly positive on both central modes. The positive observed central rate makes exclusion strict. This w=0 argument also works with component-specific fixed efficiencies, since each component's coefficients are tested separately. Under component-INDEPENDENT common mode efficiencies put w=raw ordinary fraction. At each mode the worst other coefficient is m=min(D_count,D_alt). If D_ord<0 the first possible zero of w D_ord+(1-w)m is m/(m-D_ord); other modes need no threshold. The minimum of those ratios occurs at the centre. For w below that floor all modes are nonnegative, both central modes strictly positive, contradicting the two inequalities.",
